      "new": "\\section{Mixed products from six fixed-field CM-type orbits}\n\\label{six:sec:main}\nPut $M=\\Ql(\\zeta_{42})=\\Ql(\\zeta_{21})$ and\n$G=(\\Z/42\\Z)^\\times$. As in Section~\\ref{ext:sec:main}, types refer to\nrational first cohomology: an embedding label $g$ belongs to the type\nwhen its eigenline lies in $H^{1,0}$. We use the canonical types $\\Psi_i$\nin Table~\\ref{six:tab:types}, retaining their indices in the eight-orbit\ncensus. These are abstract field markings, not additional geometric\nCM points on the McMullen curve.\n\n\\begin{theorem}[Six-orbit mixed products]\\label{six:thm:products}\nLet $X_1,\\ldots,X_N$ be complex abelian sixfolds, each with an embedding\n$M\\hookrightarrow\\End^0(X_j)$ and $\\dim_M H^1(X_j,\\Ql)=1$. Suppose each cohomological\nCM type is a Galois translate of one of the six types in\nTable~\\ref{six:tab:types}. Then every rational Hodge class on\n$X_1\\times\\cdots\\times X_N$ is algebraic, in every codimension.\nThe factors may repeat, and their lattices may be chosen independently.\nThe empty product is allowed.\n\\end{theorem}\n\n\\begin{table}[htbp]\n\\centering\\small\n\\setlength{\\tabcolsep}{4pt}\n\\begin{tabular}{r l c r r r r}\n\\toprule\n$i$ & $\\Psi_i$ & $t\\pmod{21}$ & $q$ & $\\dim A_t$ & $e$ & $h$\\\\\n\\midrule\n0 & $\\{1,5,11,13,17,19\\}$ & $(1,1,19)$ & 21 & 6 & 1 & 19\\\\\n1 & $\\{1,5,11,13,17,23\\}$ & $(1,7,13)$ & 21 & 6 & 1 & 11\\\\\n4 & $\\{1,5,11,19,25,29\\}$ & $(3,3,15)$ & 7 & 3 & 2 & 11\\\\\n5 & $\\{1,5,11,23,25,29\\}$ & $(1,6,14)$ & 21 & 6 & 1 & 1\\\\\n6 & $\\{1,11,23,25,29,37\\}$ & $(1,4,16)$ & 21 & 6 & 1 & 1\\\\\n7 & $\\{1,13,19,25,31,37\\}$ & $(7,7,7)$ & 3 & 1 & 6 & 1\\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{Degree-$21$ Fermat presentations. Here $q$ is the character\norder, $e=[M:\\Ql(\\zeta_q)]$, and $h$ transports the induced type to\n$\\Psi_i$. An admissible factor $A_t$ need not be simple.}\n\\label{six:tab:types}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\begin{proof}\nLet $F_m$ be the smooth plane Fermat curve. Its Jacobian has admissible\nrational character factors indexed by Galois orbits of triples\n$t=(t_1,t_2,t_3)$ with nonzero entries and sum zero modulo $m$.\nIf $t$ has order $q$, its factor $A_t$ has dimension $\\varphi(q)/2$\nand rank-one rational first cohomology over $E=\\Ql(\\zeta_q)$;\nno simplicity assumption is required\n\\cite[\\S2, eqs.~(2.2)--(2.4) and Definition~2.1]{Shioda1981}.\nWe specify the holomorphic convention used for these factors.\nOn $x^m+y^m=1$, the differentials\n\\[\n x^{a-1}y^{b-m}\\,dx,\n \\qquad a,b\\geq1,\\quad a+b\\leq m-1,\n\\]\nare regular and have diagonal character $(a,b,m-a-b)$.\nAt $y=0$, use $dx=-y^{m-1}x^{1-m}dy$; at infinity their local\nfactor has exponent $m-a-b-1\\geq0$. Their distinct characters and\nnumber $(m-1)(m-2)/2$ give a holomorphic basis. Thus the holomorphic\nlabels of $t$ are exactly those $u$ for which\n$\\sum_j\\langle ut_j\\rangle_m=m$, where residues lie in\n$\\{1,\\ldots,m-1\\}$, in agreement with\n\\cite[eq.~(2.1), p.~68]{Shioda1981}.\n\nFor each row of Table~\\ref{six:tab:types}, set\n$d=\\gcd(21,t_1,t_2,t_3)$, $q=21/d$, and $v=t/d$.\nThe ordered character $v$ has image $\\mu_q$. Its factor therefore\nhas the $E$-action just described, with holomorphic type\n\\[\n T_q(v)=\\left\\{u\\in(\\Z/q\\Z)^\\times:\n              \\sum_j\\langle uv_j\\rangle_q=q\\right\\}.\n\\]\nThe induced $M$-type is\n\\[\n T=\\{g\\in G:g\\bmod q\\in T_q(v)\\}\n   =\\left\\{g\\in G:\\sum_j\\langle gt_j\\rangle_{21}=21\\right\\}.\n\\]\nDirect residue calculations give $hT=\\Psi_i$ in each displayed row.\nFor example the embedding fibers have size $e=12/\\varphi(q)$,\nso the dimensions and copy multiplicities in the table follow\nwithout identifying an elliptic or three-dimensional factor with\na sixfold. The type-stabilizer orders in row order are\n$1,2,2,1,6,6$; in particular several types are induced.\n\nWe explain why induction gives an actual field action on $A_t^e$.\nWrite $V=H^1(A_t,\\Ql)$. Choosing an $E$-basis of $M$ identifies\n\\[\n H^1(A_t^e,\\Ql)=V^e\\simeq M\\otimes_E V.\n\\]\nMultiplication on the first factor is a faithful rank-one $M$-action.\nIt preserves the Hodge decomposition: after splitting $E$, a matrix\nover $E$ only mixes copies of the same embedding component, whose\nHodge type is homogeneous. The line labeled $g$ lies over the\n$E$-embedding labeled $g\\bmod q$, so its type is exactly $T$.\n\nThese rational Hodge endomorphisms are rational algebraic endomorphisms.\nIndeed, dualizing a rational Hodge map to homology gives a real linear\nmap commuting with the complex structures. Clearing denominators\nmakes it a map of lattices, hence a holomorphic homomorphism of\ncomplex tori. Its graph is algebraic for abelian varieties.\nThe reversal of composition under pullback causes no problem for\nthe commutative field $M$. Thus the construction supplies an embedding\n$M\\hookrightarrow\\End^0(A_t^e)$, not just a choice of split eigenvalues.\nRe-mark this action by $\\iota_{\\rm new}=\\iota_{\\rm old}\\circ\\sigma_h$.\nAn old label $g$ becomes $gh$, since\n$\\sigma_g(\\sigma_h(a))=\\sigma_{gh}(a)$; hence the new type is $hT$.\n\nTwo rank-one rational $M$ Hodge structures with the same type are\n$M$-linearly Hodge-isomorphic: an $M$-linear isomorphism preserves each\nembedding line and its Hodge bidegree. The weight-one lattice argument\nabove turns such an isomorphism into a complex algebraic isogeny after\nclearing denominators. This works for induced types as well as simple\nones. A further field re-marking realizes any Galois translate.\nConsequently each $X_j$ is complex-isogenous to the corresponding\n$A_t^e$. Every finite product in the theorem is therefore a degree-$21$\nFermat-type abelian variety in Shioda's sense, up to isogeny.\n\nThe remaining input is the full semigroup condition $P_{21}$.\nThe sentence preceding the published Theorem~3.3 of da Silva,\nwith its Appendix computation, establishes $P_m$ for $m=21,27$\n\\cite{daSilva2021Fermat}. We use this published result as a literature\ninput, without claiming an independent replay of its computation.\nThe superscript in the accompanying hypersurfaces $X_m^n$ denotes\ndimension, not Cartesian power. Shioda's Theorem~4.4 states that\n$P_m^{6c-2}$ implies rational Hodge algebraicity in codimension $c$\nfor every degree-$m$ Fermat-type abelian variety\n\\cite[Theorem~4.4, p.~72]{Shioda1981}.\nFull $P_{21}$ supplies this hypothesis for every $c\\geq1$.\nIt applies to the identified product, including repeated admissible\nfactors; codimension zero is immediate. This use of Theorem~4.4\nsupplies the correspondence and multiplicity statement directly,\nwithout inferring mixed algebraicity from separate factors.\nFinally, isogeny pullback and pushforward preserve cycle classes,\nwith $f_*f^*=(\\deg f)\\mathrm{id}$, and transfer the conclusion to\nthe original product over $\\Ql$.\n\\end{proof}\n\nThe finite identifications, including character kernels, embedding fibers,\nand action transporters, are retained in\n\\path{experiments/six_orbit_v109/identification.json}; the human proof\nand its primary-source hypotheses remain essential.\nTheorem~\\ref{six:thm:products} concerns the six listed isogeny classes.\nThe two remaining census types, $2$ and $3$, are addressed in\nSection~\\ref{eight:sec:main}. The six-class theorem imposes no principal polarization and\nmakes no claim of a new literal McMullen point or prescribed moduli\nnormalization. The equality $\\Ql(\\zeta_{42})=\\Ql(\\zeta_{21})$ does not\nby itself lower the order of a degree-$42$ Fermat character.\n\n\\section{Arbitrary mixed products for the fixed degree-twelve field}\n\\label{eight:sec:main}\nThe six-class result and two additional Fermat-character cycles extend\nalgebraicity to arbitrary mixed products for the fixed field\n$M=\\Ql(\\zeta_{42})$. This field restriction is essential.\n\\begin{theorem}[All mixed products for the fixed field]\\label{eight:thm:all}\nLet $X_1,\\ldots,X_N$ be complex abelian sixfolds equipped with embeddings\n$M\\hookrightarrow\\End^0(X_j)$ such that $\\dim_M H^1(X_j,\\Ql)=1$ for every $j$.\nThen every rational Hodge class on $\\prod_jX_j$ is algebraic,\nin every codimension. Repetitions, arbitrary choices of lattices and\nthe empty product are allowed.\n\\end{theorem}\n\nChoose representatives $X_i$ for the eight canonical CM-type orbits.\nBesides the six types in Table~\\ref{six:tab:types}, these are\n\\[\n \\Psi_2=\\{1,5,11,13,19,25\\}=\\Psi_{\\extD},\\qquad\n \\Psi_3=\\{1,5,11,17,23,29\\}.\n\\]\nThe eight orbits exhaust the $2^6=64$ choices of one embedding from\neach conjugate pair; their respective sizes are\n$12,6,12,12,6,12,2,2$. The marked weight-one isogeny argument in the\nproof of Theorem~\\ref{six:thm:products} reduces the theorem to arbitrary\npowers of these eight representatives, including induced types.\n\n\\subsection{Actual embedding lines and an integral relation certificate}\n\\label{eight:sec:lattice}\nSet $R=(1,5,11,13,17,19)$ and order $G=(\\Z/42\\Z)^\\times$ increasingly.\nFor every factor use the \\emph{same} row $g\\in G$ and set\n\\[\n w_{i,a}(g)=2\\mathbf1_{\\Psi_i}(ga)-1.\n\\]\nThe Hodge-torus argument of Section~\\ref{ext:sec:lattice} shows that\nzero sums of these columns are exactly the invariant signed tensor\ncharacters. All twelve actual $M$-embedding lines on each $X_i$ remain\ndistinct even if their Hodge-torus weights coincide.\nThere are $48$ actual conjugate-pair coordinates $(i,a)$, $a\\in R$.\n\nRetain the following representatives for distinct pairs of opposite\nweight columns, with the factors ordered by $i$ and the labels by size:\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|cccccccc}\ni&0&1&2&3&4&5&6&7\\\\\\hline\nR_i&R&\\{1,5,11\\}&R&R&\\{1,5,11\\}&R&\\{1\\}&\\{1\\}.\n\\end{array}\n\\]\nLet $W$ be the resulting $12\\times32$ matrix and $e_{i,a}$ its\ncoordinate vectors. Every original column is uniquely a signed retained\ncolumn of the same factor. Thus there is a surjection\n$P:\\Z^{48}\\to\\Z^{32}$ and a section $J$ including the retained actual\nlabels, with $PJ=1$ and $W_{\\rm act}=WP$. Consequently\n\\[\n \\ker_{\\Z}W_{\\rm act}\n =J(\\ker_{\\Z}W)\\oplus\\ker P.\n\\]\nThe sixteen generators of $\\ker P$ have the form\n$f_{i,a}-\\epsilon f_{i,b}$, where $a$ is not retained and\n$w_{i,a}=\\epsilon w_{i,b}$. Their tensors use two singleton copies:\n$L_{i,a}\\otimes L_{i,-b}$ if $\\epsilon=1$, and\n$L_{i,a}\\otimes L_{i,b}$ if $\\epsilon=-1$.\nThey are split components of rational degree-two Hodge classes and are\nalgebraic by Lefschetz $(1,1)$. This justifies the reduction without\nidentifying different geometric eigenlines or assuming they are\ninterchanged by a prescribed endomorphism.\n\nHere is a precise finite certificate for the remaining integer kernel.\nAll coordinate indices in this paragraph are \\emph{zero-based}.\nThe pivot columns of $W$ are $(0,1,2,3,4,6)$ and its rank is six.\nWrite $F$ for the increasing list of the other $26$ indices.\nThe unique kernel chart $K$ whose rows in $F$ form the identity\nhas integral entries. Thus $\\ker_{\\Z}W=K\\Z^{26}$.\nFor $S=\\{0,1,4,5,6,7\\}$, use the same column order on $W_S$;\nits analogous free-coordinate kernel chart has fourteen columns.\nEmbed these fourteen columns in $\\Z^{32}$, and follow them by the\nnine vectors in \\eqref{ext:eq:basis}, embedding $B,{\\extD},Y$\nrespectively in factors $0,2,4$ with the displayed retained labels.\nCall this ordered list of $23$ known vectors $\\mathcal B$.\nThey have rank $19$ and are algebraic by\nTheorems~\\ref{six:thm:products} and~\\ref{ext:thm:mixed}; here\n$X_4$ is isogenous to $Y^2$.\n\nDefine two further relations\n\\begin{align*}\n q&=-e_{0,1}-e_{0,17}+e_{1,1}+e_{2,1},\\\\\n r&= e_{0,1}-e_{0,5}+e_{0,13}-2e_{1,1}+e_{3,1}.\n\\end{align*}\nFor $h\\in G$, send each label $a$ to $ha$ and then reduce to its\nsigned representative using $P$. Order the distinct resulting integer\nvectors lexicographically in the fixed $32$ coordinates. Each of\n$q$ and $r$ has twelve such translates. Form a square matrix by taking,\nin order, the projections to $F$ of:\n\\begin{enumerate}[label=(\\roman*)]\n\\item the first nineteen vectors of $\\mathcal B$ (the fourteen\n$S$-chart columns followed by the first five rows of\n\\eqref{ext:eq:basis});\n\\item the lexicographically first translate of $q$;\n\\item the first six lexicographically ordered translates of $r$.\n\\end{enumerate}\nIts determinant is $-1$. Its retained integer inverse, multiplied by\nthe corresponding full $32$-coordinate matrix, gives exactly $K$.\nThese explicitly specified finite identities are certified in\n\\path{experiments/all_eight_v109/lattice.json}, reconstructed by\n\\path{experiments/all_eight_v109/review/independent_check.py}, and\nregressed in \\path{tests/test_v109_reductions.py}.\nThe chart identity proves integral surjectivity onto the free\ncoordinates, while unimodularity proves that the known vectors and\nthe two seed orbits generate the full integer kernel. Equal rational\nrank alone is not used to infer saturation.\n\n\\subsection{Two explicit algebraic source tensors}\n\\label{eight:sec:sources}\nEach occurrence below has its own marked source copy. A negative sign\nin a relation means the conjugate embedding; the coefficient $-2$ in\n$r$ requires two distinct copies of $X_1$.\nThe following source presentations have exactly the indicated canonical\ncohomological types, with nonzero entries and character order $42$:\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c|c}\n\\text{relation}&\\text{factor}&\\text{Fermat triple}\\\\\\hline\nq&0&(1,1,40)\\\\\nq,r&1&(1,13,28)\\\\\nq&2&(1,14,27)\\\\\nr&0&(1,20,21)\\\\\nr&3&(1,6,35).\n\\end{array}\n\\]\nIndeed $\\sum_j\\langle gt_j\\rangle_{42}=42$ selects $\\Psi_i$\nfor each row. Each associated rational character factor has rank-one\n$M$ first cohomology and dimension six; the type-$1$ factor need not\nbe simple. The marked isogeny argument above identifies it with the\nrequired $X_i$. The source embeddings for $q$ are\n$(0,41),(0,25),(1,1),(2,1)$; those for $r$ are\n$(0,1),(0,37),(0,13),(1,41),(1,41),(3,1)$, with the repeated\npair occupying different copies.\n\nConcatenate the four triples after their indicated multipliers for $q$.\nThe resulting multiset has the following partition:\n\\[\n (1,41),\\quad(1,41),\\quad(2,13,25,25,27,34),\\quad(14,28).\n\\]\nFor the six source copies of $r$ a partition is\n\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\n (1,41),\\quad(1,41),\\quad(6,20,21,37),\\\\\n (8,21,26,29),\\quad(13,29),\\quad(14,14,21,35).\n\\end{gathered}\n\\]\nEvery block $C$ is balanced:\n$\\sum_{a\\in C}\\langle ga\\rangle_{42}=21|C|$ for every $g\\in G$.\nThe only length-six block splits into the two nonzero zero-sum triples\n$(2,13,27)$ and $(25,25,34)$. Thus the blocks satisfy exactly\nShioda's length-two and length-four conditions and, for length six,\nhis additional product-of-triples condition\n\\cite[Theorem~4.3 and Lemmas~4.1--4.2, pp.~70--72]{Shioda1981}.\nThat theorem has no odd-degree restriction; it applies at level $42$.\nWe use its proof supportwise for these two characters, rather than\nasserting that every level-$42$ invariant support has such a partition.\nThe permitted block eigenspaces are algebraic, and the algebraic\ninduction correspondences combine them into the concatenated line.\n\nThe algebraic correspondences in those lemmas transfer the supported\nFermat-character cycles to the nonzero tensor components of the marked\ncurve products. For $q$ the concatenation has twelve entries, hence\nFermat dimension ten and Hodge type $(5,5)$; the corresponding curve\ntensor has type $(2,2)$. For $r$ the Fermat dimension is sixteen and the curve tensor has\ncohomological degree six, with types $(8,8)$ and $(3,3)$ respectively. These are the required\nTate shifts, not degree-preserving identifications. Passing through\nJacobian $H^1$, the rational character projectors, and the marked\nisogenies gives the desired nonzero singleton-copy tensors on the\n$X_i$. All projectors are algebraic correspondences with coefficients\nin the splitting field $M$. Distinct source copies keep repeated\ncharacters in different exterior positions. Nonzero transfer follows\nfrom the finite-degree dominant maps in Lemma~4.2: their pullback of\na marked summand is a sum of products of the curve-character lines,\nand pushforward divided by the map degree recovers the original class.\nThe split line and the algebraic subspace are $M$-defined; inclusion\nafter extension to $\\C$ descends faithfully to $M$. An individual\nnonrational eigenline is therefore not being called a rational cycle.\nThe exact multiset, all twelve balance tests, actual CM types and the\nsix-block splitting are independently checked by\n\\path{experiments/all_eight_v109/validate_witness.py}.\n\n\\subsection{Integral combinations, contractions and rational descent}\n\\begin{proof}[Proof of Theorem~\\ref{eight:thm:all}]\nWork temporarily with cycle-class coefficients extended from $\\Ql$ to\n$M$. Rational polynomials in multiplication-by-$n$ pullback isolate\n$H^1$; actual $M$-endomorphisms then give the individual embedding-line\nprojectors. They distinguish all actual lines, even when their Hodge\nweights agree. Addition pullback and these projectors separate any\nsource wedge into a specified nonzero singleton-copy tensor.\nConversely diagonal pullback assembles singleton factors into any\nlegitimate target exterior monomial.\n\nWe need nonzero contractions for actual conjugate embedding pairs.\nChoose an ample rational divisor $\\theta$ on each sixfold $X_i$.\nOn two singleton copies the operation\n\\[\n (x,y)\\longmapsto\\int_{X_i}x\\smile y\\smile\\theta^5\n\\]\nis algebraic: diagonal pullback, cup product and proper pushforward.\nIt is nonzero on $L_{i,a}\\otimes L_{i,-a}$ by the polarization's\nRiemann bilinear positivity applied to $v$ and $\\overline v$.\nThis remains valid for induced types with repeated weights;\nnondegeneracy of the whole pairing alone would not establish that\nindividual assertion. No inverse-Lefschetz correspondence or principal\npolarization is needed.\n\nSuppose algebraic singleton tensors represent signed relations\n$v_j$ and their negatives. For an integer expression\n$z=\\sum_j n_jv_j$, take external products, choosing $-v_j$ when\n$n_j<0$. In each actual coordinate contract all opposite pairs.\nThe surviving tensor has the signed multiplicities of $z$ and is a\nnonzero scalar multiple of its canonical tensor. It remains algebraic\nwith coefficients in $M$. This proves closure under arbitrary signed\ninteger combinations, without a conformal decomposition or extraction\nof roots of cycle classes.\n\nThe known classes and the two source tensors therefore give their\nGalois translates. Here coefficient Galois action preserves the\nrational cycle-class image and translates all embedding labels\nsimultaneously. An actual translate need not equal $J$ of its reduced\ntranslate, but their difference lies in $\\ker P$. The sixteen divisor\nrelations and the contraction argument correct this difference.\nThe integer certificate then supplies every reduced kernel relation,\nand the split exact sequence supplies every actual kernel relation.\n\nFinally take an invariant exterior monomial on any requested product.\nRemove matched occurrences of opposite actual labels, accounting for\nthem by divisor classes. The remaining signed vector belongs to\n$\\ker_{\\Z}W_{\\rm act}$ and hence has an algebraic singleton tensor.\nRestore the removed pairs and assemble the tensor into the target\ncopies by diagonals. No duplicate identical embedding appears within\none target exterior factor, so the result is nonzero. Auxiliary source\ntypes absent from the target are contracted away completely.\nAll invariant split monomials are thus algebraic. They span the\nextension to $M$ of the rational Hodge space; faithful scalar extension\nimplies equality of its rational subspace with the rational cycle-class\nimage. Complex isogeny invariance gives the stated arbitrary factors.\n\\end{proof}\n\nThis conclusion is limited to rank-one first cohomology over the fixed\nfield $M$. It neither asserts the Hodge conjecture for all CM fields\nnor identifies any new geometric McMullen point, polarized isomorphism,\nor principally polarized moduli normalization.\n\n\\begin{corollary}[CM factors with fields contained in the fixed field]\n\\label{eight:cor:subfields}\nLet $A$ be a complex abelian variety. Suppose that every simple factor $B$\nin a complex isogeny decomposition of $A$ admits an embedding\n$E\\hookrightarrow\\End^0(B)$ of a CM field with $[E:\\Ql]=2\\dim B$,\nand that $E$ admits a $\\Ql$-field embedding into\n$M=\\Ql(\\zeta_{21})$. Then every rational Hodge class on $A$ is algebraic.\nThe same holds for arbitrary finite mixed products of varieties satisfying\nthis hypothesis, including all their powers.\n\\end{corollary}\n\\begin{proof}\nWrite $A\\sim\\prod_j B_j^{m_j}$ over $\\C$, and identify each chosen\n$E_j$ with its image in $M$. Set $e_j=[M:E_j]$ and $X_j=B_j^{e_j}$.\nThen $\\dim X_j=6$. If $V_j=H^1(B_j,\\Ql)$, the full-degree CM action\nmakes $V_j$ rank one over $E_j$, and an $E_j$-basis of $M$ identifies\n\\[\n H^1(X_j,\\Ql)=V_j^{e_j}\\simeq M\\otimes_{E_j}V_j.\n\\]\nMultiplication on the first factor gives a faithful rank-one $M$-action\npreserving the Hodge decomposition. Its type is the inverse image of the\n$E_j$-type under restriction of embeddings. The weight-one lattice argument\nin the proof of Theorem~\\ref{six:thm:products} realizes this action in\n$\\End^0(X_j)$; maximal-order integrality on the original lattice is not\nrequired. Thus every $X_j$ meets Theorem~\\ref{eight:thm:all}.\n\nChoose $N_j$ with $e_jN_j\\geq m_j$. Theorem~\\ref{eight:thm:all} gives\nrational Hodge algebraicity on\n$Z=\\prod_j X_j^{N_j}=\\prod_j B_j^{e_jN_j}$.\nProjection onto $A'=\\prod_j B_j^{m_j}$ has an algebraic section inserting\norigins in the extra factors. For a Hodge class $\\alpha$ on $A'$, pullback\nto $Z$ is algebraic; pullback along the section recovers $\\alpha$ and\npreserves algebraic cycle classes. Complex isogeny invariance transfers\nthis conclusion to $A$. A mixed product of varieties satisfying the\nhypothesis has simple factors satisfying the same hypothesis, so the\nargument also proves the last assertion. We use the specific mixed-product\ntheorem above, not a general closure of the Hodge conjecture under products.\n\\end{proof}\nThe field hypotheses in Corollary~\\ref{eight:cor:subfields} are explicit:\nthey concern full-degree CM fields acting by rational endomorphisms and\nembedding into $M$. No equivalence with a Mumford--Tate splitting-field\ncondition, or assertion for arbitrary CM fields, is claimed.\n\n\\part{Audit of the published Hecke reduction}",
